\title{Definitions as Queries}
\import{prelude}

\scope{
\put\transclude/toc{false}
\subtree[001A]{

  \taxon{Slogan}
  \title{Sets are queries, elements are results}

  \p{A \em{query} on a theory is a way of turning a model of the theory (i.e. actual database instance) into a set. Applying this to a particular model gives an actual set, and elements of that set are the results of the query.}
}}

\p{That is, a query on some theory \code{T} is just definition that has the following shape:}

\pre{%
\kw{def} my-query (m : T) : \kw{Set} := ...
}

\p{Here are some simple cases. Given database containing a graph, we might want to query for the full set of vertices. For this, we can project the field of the model directly.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Graph := \kw{sig}
  V : Set
  E : V -> V -> Set
\kw{end}

\kw{def} its-vertices (m : Graph) : \kw{Set} := m.V
}

\p{Or, to constrain the results according to the value of a column, we might apply a function that returns a set:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Layered := \kw{sig}
  layer : Int -> Set
\kw{end}

\kw{def} for-millenium (m : Layered) : \kw{Set} := m.layer 2000
}

\p{For more complicated queries, we can use the type constructors we've seen.}

\transclude{001J}
